we begin today in europe where a  national lockdown has gone into effect  in austria this is in addition to  restrictions in other countries such as  in the netherlands and belgium to  control rising case numbers primarily to  do with lower than expected vaccination  figures but these restrictions have  prompted protests and in some cases  violence such as in belgium where police  clashed with protesters on sunday there  is anger over the government's advice to  get vaccinated and any possible moves to  impose mandatory vaccinations as austria  has done  similar scenes were reported in the  netherlands which saw a third straight  night of violence  now getting people to agree to vaccinate  themselves has been hard vaccination  figures vary across europe from 67 in  germany to a little over 81 percent in  portugal and people have varying reasons  for not wanting to vaccinate against  covet 19.  we want our freedom and being injected  by something that's not tested well um  being um not able to work where you want  to work to be where you want to be  that's not what we stand for that's not  freedom that's not where we live live in  west europe and we just want to be free  like we were before it's saying  they're young people  and they've had little freedom for  almost two years now because of covet  19.  so yes i understand in a way  but i don't approve  to make a point to stand for our freedom  and to demand for more  open communication from the government  and  to to the civilians  from the politicians especially from the  scientists that they make clear stances  and be clear and fair with us and also  respect our opinion  which is  some of us we don't want to get  vaccinated  we're being put under pressure now and  ultimately we're no longer free i'll  submit to the pressure but honestly i'm  convinced mandatory vaccination is  coming  so while many in europe believe their  freedoms are compromised through  vaccination in most places in asia it's  just the opposite many believe  vaccination is the key to their freedom  which is why some nations in the region  rank among the world's best when it  comes to vaccination rates countries  like japan cambodia and malaysia  have also started reopening even as  europe starts to reclose  remember back to when the tokyo olympic  games were about to open and some  japanese  we're out on the streets saying cancel  the summer games covet 19 rates were  skyrocketing in japan and vaccination  numbers were low  fast forward to late autumn japan's  vaccination rate has finally taken off  whatever  vaccination is voluntary in japan so i  think it's a big thing that people go to  the vaccination site because they want  to receive it the inoculation completion  rate has reached about 80 percent and i  think that's the reason why  by the end of the year japan should be  near 85 vaccinated with bonus points for  an extraordinary percentage of people  wearing masks compared to much of the  rest of the world  this summer malaysia and indonesia were  battling some of the highest covet 19  case loads as the delta variant tore  through the region  both have managed to stem the surge with  malaysia now claiming 76 of its  population fully vaccinated  obviously is a lot of improvement  a lot of improvement in cambodia as well  one of the poorer countries in the  region but it's keeping up thanks to  vaccine procurement  its efforts to counter misinformation  and a general okay with old and young  getting the jab  i brought her to get vaccinated today  because i want my daughter to be free  from kovitz  also she'll be attending school soon she  shouldn't give it to her classmates nor  they to her  some nations continue to struggle india  may have hit the billion vaccination  mark but it's only 29  fully inoculated among adults  taiwan is well under 50 but the island  now has upped its game on vaccine  procurement and expects to see those  numbers rise in the coming months  and joining me now for more are  journalists michael penn in tokyo and  joyce lee from dw's east asia bureau in  taipei welcome to you both michael to  you first now japan was a slow starter  when it came to vaccinations but the  picture is very different now why  well i think this is really attributable  to the sort of japanese decision making  process and just kind of the behavior of  japanese bureaucracies in general  oftentimes they're very slow to make  decisions this is hardly unusual  to feel secure about something to  establish procedures to get everybody to  kind of agree to how to do something and  that often takes a lot longer than it  would take in most other countries but  on the other hand what japanese do very  well is after all the decisions been  made after all the buy-in is there  implementation is then smooth and  methodical and that's really what we've  seen with the vaccinations in japan slow  start but then after that just uh chugga  chugga chugga straight through to where  we are now  what about the buy-in from  the citizenry is that something that has  uh helped in the process  uh certainly uh you know uh unlike i  guess a lot of other places you know the  the whole vaccination issue whether or  not to be vaccinated  isn't really a political issue here  there's no there's no culture war divide  about you know as there is in some  countries between let's say people on  the left and people on the right about  about whether or not to get vaccinated  or not it just isn't politicized i think  most japanese kind of have a general  trust of the medical profession and so  they're uh they're willing to go along  with it maybe they didn't want to be the  first ones to get vaccinated but once  they saw that other people are getting  vaccinated and they were okay  they were okay to go and get vaccinated  themselves  joyce to you in taipei now the taiwanese  were also hesitant to vaccinate  themselves especially in the summer  what's the situation now  well um  almost 50 of the population are fully  vaccinated now and that's a huge leap  compared to uh what happened in the  summer before the major outbreak  happened in may people were  very hesitant and  they were very concerned about the side  effects of the vaccines especially when  there was simply  almost no covet in taiwan at that time  so uh people didn't feel the urgency to  get vaccinated and even the government  the authority said that there was no  hurry to purchase vaccines because uh  the containment of the virus had been so  successful at that time but after the  major outbreak in may people were very  scared of getting infected and  they start  they started to once you get vaccinated  and desperate for vaccines so the number  is going up now and  the supply of vaccines is also getting  better now  talk to us a bit about the role of the  government here in trying to convince  people to get vaccinated and the reason  i ask this is because here in europe at  least governments have been unable to  convince large numbers of people to  follow restrictions or for that matter  get themselves vaccinated  well it's not that hard for the  government to convince people to follow  the rules and the restrictions uh  because um we we should uh not ignore  the cultural nuance uh here in taiwan or  in chinese society there's a saying that  goes uh sacrifice oneself for the  greater good uh for the society so um  it's a collectivist uh society here in  taiwan and uh  people are more willing to sacrifice  their freedom  or their interests in order to protect  the people around them and bear in mind  that taiwanese experienced the sas  outbreak in 2003 so wearing a mask  was uh very taken for granted um  uh  even before the mass mandate taiwanese  people were rushing to uh convenience  stores to get their mosque uh to protect  themselves and the people around them  and wearing a mask not wearing a mask is  like not wearing pens in public nowadays  so and there's a peer pressure in taiwan  that if you don't  follow what the majority people do  you kind of get weird looks from others  so uh this is the cultural uh side of um  uh what we're facing here in taiwan  uh michael to you in tokyo uh joyce was  just talking about the cultural nuance  essentially the shame almost  if you don't wear if you're not wearing  a mask it's akin to not wearing pants i  mean is that something that one could  apply to japan as well which would sort  of explain uh why japan has been able to  control uh kovit 19 much better  particularly after the olympics  yeah almost everything joyce said there  uh applies with almost equal force with  japan uh you know the culture uh  believes in in cooperation and doing  things for the community much as she uh  outlined in taiwan uh maybe the only two  differences would be  that uh you know we didn't have the sars  outbreak here so there wasn't uh that uh  and also that uh you know there was also  not a really a lull in the covid  outbreak we have had five waves here in  japan which have been pretty consistent  so uh whereas she said taiwan is about  fifty percent vaccinated here in japan  we're close to eighty percent vaccinated  one i think it's perhaps the highest  rate among g7 or g  countries as far as i know but anyway uh  it's uh it's gone forward steadily and  it continues i mean even though the  rates of daily vaccinations is down  that's probably just because we're  getting to the parts of the population  where very few people need to be  vaccinated among 65 and olders  it's 92 percent of the japanese  population is now vaccinated  i mean numbers that people in uh europe  will only be jealous of i have to ask  you michael we don't have much time left  but how do people in japan view what's  happening in europe in terms of the  protests and the violence that one is  seeing against against  government measures and restrictions  well honestly i imagine most japanese  kind of shrug at it and kind of say well  that there's foreigners being violent  again i mean there's kind of this  feeling in japan that that oversees  people you know make trouble and make  hassles over things that they don't  really need to make hassles over so i  think they probably look at it and say  yep those are the foreigners that's what  they do you know good thing we don't  have that here  journalist michael penley in tokyo and  dw correspondent joyce lee in taipei  thanks very much both of you for joining  us today

